00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:07.840
opens up for enterprise users
is through Google Cloud.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:10.240
So what is a TPU?

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:14.900
This is basically the schematic
of what a TPU looks like.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:18.670
So you basically have
two cores per chip.

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:23.140
And each core basically contains
a matrix multiplication unit.

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:24.380
That's the heart of it.

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:26.590
It also contains scalar
and vector units.

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:27.820
I'll talk about those.

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:30.380
And it contains about
8 GBs of memory.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:32.200
I'm talking about
TPU version two.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:33.490
Version three has more memory.

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:34.690
It has 32 GBs.

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:37.080
But version two
has 8 GBs of memory

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:39.890
per core, which, in the larger
scheme of things, seems tiny.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:40.390
Right.

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:42.669
Why would you build a chip
with just 8 GBs of memory?

00:08:42.669 --> 00:08:45.940
But it turns out that it's
not about this one core.

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:48.430
It's about basically how
many you can put together.

00:08:48.430 --> 00:08:52.570
So per chip, you
basically have, now,

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:56.080
per core you basically
have all of these things.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:00.190
And you have about
8 GB memory cores.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:02.250
Four of these chips per TPU.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:04.210
Each chip contains two cores.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:07.960
So you have a total
of eight cores on one

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:09.980
of these TPU boards.

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:12.160
But then you take
these TPU boards,

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:14.420
and you put them
together into a rack.

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:14.920
Right.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:16.540
And that's what we call a pod.

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:19.180
And a pod has 64 TPUs.

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:22.330
And each of those TPUs has
four chips, has two cores.

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:25.630
So that's basically getting
the power out there.

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:26.220
So great.

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:28.450
So you have 64 TPUs per pod.

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:32.560
And the matrix multiplication
is where all of the power

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:33.310
comes in.

00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:37.100
The idea is that you go
look at machine learning.

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:38.670
And what is all of
machine learning?

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:41.950
It's essentially linear
algebra, matrix multiplication.

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:47.710
So you build a system that does
matrix multiplication very fast

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:49.700
and nothing else.

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:51.940
And that's basically
the idea behind a TPU.

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:57.020
Just do matrix multiplication
really, really, really fast.

00:09:57.020 --> 00:10:01.400
So the idea is to basically
pack a lot of power and density,

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:03.370
because it doesn't have
to do anything else.

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:05.540
All it does is matrix
multiplication.

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:09.400
And if we can do that, the
idea is it's a fixed function

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:10.310
architecture.

00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:13.120
And if you were
to think of a CPU

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:18.160
as doing scalar math, a
GPU of doing vector math,

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:19.930
a TPU is doing Tensor math.

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:22.060
Right.

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:25.840
And it's just focusing
on matrices and matrix

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:27.550
multiplication.

00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:32.890
The second part of this is
that all of those chips,

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:34.120
they aren't individual.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:38.410
They're all connected by a
very high speed toroidal mesh.

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:40.510
And what this essentially
means is that they

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:43.250
can function as one unit.

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:45.900
So you're not actually
treating one core.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.620
Actually you're treating
all of them together.

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:50.290
So that's great.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:53.200
But what that actually means
is that as you increase

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:56.680
the number of cores, the
number of images that you're

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:59.590
processing, or the number of
texts that you're processing

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:01.080
grows linearly.

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:06.446
This is a very, very,
very hard graph to get.

00:11:06.446 --> 00:11:07.570
We're all familiar with it.

00:11:07.570 --> 00:11:08.110
Right?

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:09.160
You distribute your code.

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:10.810
What happens to your code?

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:12.210
It doesn't scale linearly.

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:12.710
Right.

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:14.440
It slows down.

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:17.090
You're the fastest
if you use just one.

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:21.150
With the TPU the idea
that one is a pod.

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:24.600
As long as you're within
the pod, it's just as fast.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:27.030
So you're basically getting
that high speed interconnect

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:29.850
so that you're getting
the scaling that is pretty

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:32.310
linear for the number of cores.

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:35.153
AUDIENCE: You're not concerned
about the clock speed, as well

00:11:35.153 --> 00:11:36.545
as the feature size?

00:11:36.545 --> 00:11:37.420
LAK LAKSHMANAN: Yeah.

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:39.336
So all of those things
we are concerned about.

00:11:39.336 --> 00:11:40.620
And those are optimized.

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:43.230
But every chip is optimizing it.

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:45.750
The key idea here
is we're focused

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:47.230
on matrix multiplication.

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:49.440
And we'll make sure
that this thing will

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:53.550
be focused on the networking
so that this thing scales

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:55.470
linearly.

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:57.090
I think it's a
Google philosophy.

00:11:57.090 --> 00:11:57.590
Right?

