00:19:31.262 --> 00:19:33.700
And now I'm
downloading the data.

00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:37.810
And having gotten it, I
have x train and y test.

00:19:37.810 --> 00:19:43.810
And I then have a function to
do my fit, my fit and my train

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:44.660
function.

00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:47.380
So what I'm doing is I'm
taking the image that I get in,

00:19:47.380 --> 00:19:48.910
and basically flattening it out.

00:19:48.910 --> 00:19:53.500
I have a 28x28 image in MNIST,
making that a 784 array.

00:19:53.500 --> 00:19:56.770
And then here take my
weights, which is a variable,

00:19:56.770 --> 00:19:59.570
taking my bias, which
is another variable,

00:19:59.570 --> 00:20:03.350
and then doing a very fancy
logistic regression model.

00:20:03.350 --> 00:20:05.230
So x times w plus b.

00:20:05.230 --> 00:20:05.730
Right.

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:07.010
That's my model.

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:09.040
And then my loss.

00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:11.780
So this is the one change
that you have to make.

00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:14.020
So my loss is normally
by cross entropy.

00:20:14.020 --> 00:20:17.470
And my optimizer basically
optimizes the loss.

00:20:17.470 --> 00:20:19.240
I can use an atom optimizer.

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:22.240
But you don't use the
atom optimizer directly.

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:25.330
What you do is that you
take that atom optimizer,

00:20:25.330 --> 00:20:30.040
and you wrap it up in what is
called a cross shard optimizer.

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:33.700
Remember what I said about a
TPU basically scales linearly

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:35.430
with the number of cores?

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:38.091
The cross shard optimizer
is what does that.

00:20:38.091 --> 00:20:38.590
Right.

00:20:38.590 --> 00:20:41.780
So instead of it optimizing
on that one core,

00:20:41.780 --> 00:20:45.440
it's going to take this batch of
images that's going to come in.

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:47.440
It's going to spread them
out onto how many ever

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:48.810
cores you have.

00:20:48.810 --> 00:20:52.450
And each of the cores is
going to do this optimization

00:20:52.450 --> 00:20:55.030
on its part of the batch.

00:20:55.030 --> 00:20:56.710
And then there is a barrier.

00:20:56.710 --> 00:20:58.720
And everything hits the barrier.

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:00.760
All of the gradients
get averaged.

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:03.590
And then you're on
to the next step.

00:21:03.590 --> 00:21:07.180
So the cross shard optimizer
is the one change you typically

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:10.390
have to make to your code
to basically take advantage

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:11.900
of this linear scaling.

00:21:11.900 --> 00:21:13.690
So we do this, and
then we say, OK, I want

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:15.300
to go ahead and optimize it.

00:21:15.300 --> 00:21:18.290
So at this point then,
we can basically say,

00:21:18.290 --> 00:21:22.250
I want to take my fifth batch
function and rewrite it.

00:21:22.250 --> 00:21:24.920
TPU rewrite of the
fifth batch function.

00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:28.600
So my original function
would run on a CPU or a GPU.

00:21:28.600 --> 00:21:31.606
Rewrite it so that it
can also run on a TPU.

00:21:31.606 --> 00:21:35.330
And run it for one step.

00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:36.700
Actually run for 50 steps.

00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:38.600
Range of 50.

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:39.100
OK.

00:21:39.100 --> 00:21:42.780
And then you basically
get your losses.

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:44.290
So that's pretty much it.

00:21:44.290 --> 00:21:45.960
So you can do
iterative development

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:48.570
with a TPU attached
to your machine.

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:52.770
And you're writing
a Jupyter notebook.

00:21:52.770 --> 00:21:56.460
The way you're familiar with
