So what I can then do
is to basically go ahead

00:35:26.330 --> 00:35:32.570
and do GCloud ML Engine,
pointing at the core, which

00:35:32.570 --> 00:35:36.320
essentially comes from the
repo that I just showed you,

00:35:36.320 --> 00:35:41.030
saying, please go ahead
and run this on a TPU

00:35:41.030 --> 00:35:43.580
using TensorFlow version
1.8, because that

00:35:43.580 --> 00:35:45.380
was the one I tested it on.

00:35:45.380 --> 00:35:49.070
And with these many images.

00:35:49.070 --> 00:35:51.470
So this is the one thing
that you have to change.

00:35:51.470 --> 00:35:53.780
Figure out how many
images you have.

00:35:53.780 --> 00:35:55.460
And pre-process it.

00:35:55.460 --> 00:35:56.330
Run it.

00:35:56.330 --> 00:35:57.890
You have a trained model.

00:35:57.890 --> 00:36:00.140
And then once you have
your trained model,

00:36:00.140 --> 00:36:02.020
you can deploy it on ML Engine.

00:36:02.020 --> 00:36:03.890
You can deploy
anywhere you want.

00:36:03.890 --> 00:36:06.200
And then you can just
invoke the model.

00:36:06.200 --> 00:36:07.962
It's just a Python program.

00:36:07.962 --> 00:36:10.170
And you should basically
get back the classification.

00:36:10.170 --> 00:36:11.391
Send in a JPEG file.

00:36:11.391 --> 00:36:12.890
So when you're
invoking the program,

00:36:12.890 --> 00:36:14.380
you don't need
TensorFlow records.

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:16.010
So just send in a JPEG file.

00:36:16.010 --> 00:36:19.060
You get back the classification.

00:36:19.060 --> 00:36:22.620
So questions?

00:36:22.620 --> 00:36:26.570
So bottom line then, state
of the art image model

00:36:26.570 --> 00:36:27.891
pretty much all done.

00:36:27.891 --> 00:36:28.390
Right.

00:36:28.390 --> 00:36:30.920
So essentially
run a few scripts.

00:36:30.920 --> 00:36:32.150
Take your data.

00:36:32.150 --> 00:36:34.820
Make sure your data
is in that CSV format.

00:36:34.820 --> 00:36:35.570
Right.

00:36:35.570 --> 00:36:36.530
Name of the file.

00:36:36.530 --> 00:36:37.340
Category.

00:36:37.340 --> 00:36:41.641
And follow the code lab,
and you should have it done.

00:36:41.641 --> 00:36:42.140
Yes.

00:36:42.140 --> 00:36:47.244
AUDIENCE: Should you delete all
your TensorFlow data before,

00:36:47.244 --> 00:36:48.785
because that takes
up all this space?

00:36:48.785 --> 00:36:51.565
You expand on these JPEG images?

00:36:51.565 --> 00:36:52.940
LAK LAKSHMANAN:
We don't actually

00:36:52.940 --> 00:36:54.320
expand on the JPEG images.

00:36:54.320 --> 00:36:56.270
The JPEG images in
the TensorFlow record

00:36:56.270 --> 00:36:57.560
remains compressed.

00:36:57.560 --> 00:36:59.972
It remains the JPEG bytes data.

00:36:59.972 --> 00:37:01.430
The only thing that
we are doing is

00:37:01.430 --> 00:37:04.830
we are taking the JPEG bytes
data that's in those files.

00:37:04.830 --> 00:37:07.040
And instead of it
reading individual files,

00:37:07.040 --> 00:37:08.600
it's going to read
one TF record.

00:37:08.600 --> 00:37:11.330
It's going to get 1,000 of
them, along with the label.

00:37:11.330 --> 00:37:11.830
Right.

00:37:11.830 --> 00:37:12.371
So it's good.

00:37:12.371 --> 00:37:13.910
That's essentially
what we've done.

00:37:13.910 --> 00:37:17.300
We've basically minimized
the amount of IO hit

00:37:17.300 --> 00:37:18.100
that has to happen.

00:37:18.100 --> 00:37:18.660
AUDIENCE: Not expanding.

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:19.080
LAK LAKSHMANAN: Yeah.

00:37:19.080 --> 00:37:20.710
It's not uncompressing the data.

00:37:20.710 --> 00:37:23.350
No.

00:37:23.350 --> 00:37:23.850
Yes.

00:37:23.850 --> 00:37:28.730
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] so
much faster afterwards?

00:37:28.730 --> 00:37:29.730
LAK LAKSHMANAN: Exactly.

00:37:29.730 --> 00:37:30.229
Yes.

00:37:30.229 --> 00:37:30.900
Absolutely.

00:37:30.900 --> 00:37:33.860
As you pointed out, even though
you have to do this conversion,

00:37:33.860 --> 00:37:34.920
it is so much faster.

00:37:34.920 --> 00:37:37.080
Because you do the
conversion only once.

00:37:37.080 --> 00:37:40.860
But you train so
many epochs, right.

00:37:40.860 --> 00:37:45.060
Rather than doing the conversion
75,000 times because you're

00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:47.769
doing 75,000 now,
whatever, you get

00:37:47.769 --> 00:37:49.060
to do the conversion only once.

00:37:49.060 --> 00:37:51.120
So pay the penalty once.

00:37:51.120 --> 00:37:55.140
And that penalty also, you
can scale it differently.

00:37:55.140 --> 00:37:59.520
You can run the Dataflow job on
50 machines, where you may be

00:37:59.520 --> 00:38:02.090
training it only on two TPUs.

00:38:02.090 --> 00:38:05.220
So instead of you doing
this thing on 16 cores,

00:38:05.220 --> 00:38:07.800
you now suddenly did
it on many more cores,

00:38:07.800 --> 00:38:09.540
because hey, CPUs are cheap.

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:11.430
So do it on a lot of CPUs.

00:38:16.918 --> 00:38:21.450
So let's flip from state
of the art image model.

00:38:21.450 --> 00:38:23.040
So you want to train
an image model.

00:38:23.040 --> 00:38:24.360
Pretty much take the code.

00:38:24.360 --> 00:38:26.070
Run it on your data.

00:38:26.070 --> 00:38:26.810
And it's trained.

00:38:26.810 --> 00:38:27.309
Right.

00:38:27.309 --> 00:38:31.140
So basically the choices
that you have to make

00:38:31.140 --> 00:38:33.880
are essentially how big of
an image model do you want?

00:38:33.880 --> 00:38:36.360
Do you want Resonant
50, or Resonant 18,

00:38:36.360 --> 00:38:38.370
or Resonant 120 something?

00:38:38.370 --> 00:38:40.980
Depending on the
number of images,

00:38:40.980 --> 00:38:42.810
you can go deeper and deeper.

00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:44.787
So that's essentially
the only thing

00:38:44.787 --> 00:38:45.870
that you might want to do.

00:38:45.870 --> 00:38:47.730
You might want to hyper
parameter tune it.

00:38:47.730 --> 00:38:48.960
And that's fine as well.

00:38:48.960 --> 00:38:49.560
Right.

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:52.350
All of these models support
hyper parameter tuning.

00:38:52.350 --> 00:38:54.770
So you can basically say, don't
just train the model once.

00:38:54.770 --> 00:38:57.280
Train it with all these
different parameters,

00:38:57.280 --> 00:39:02.290
including the depth
of the Resonant model.

00:39:02.290 --> 00:39:05.250
So let's change a little
bit and say, how would we do

00:39:05.250 --> 00:39:07.170
state of the art text models?

00:39:07.170 --> 00:39:08.550
OK.

00:39:08.550 --> 00:39:11.700
Unlike image models, with
text it really matters.

00:39:11.700 --> 00:39:12.770
Right.

00:39:12.770 --> 00:39:14.740
An image is a consistent format.

00:39:14.740 --> 00:39:16.090
Everything is the same.

00:39:16.090 --> 00:39:18.601
You're just basically
training an image model.

00:39:18.601 --> 00:39:20.100
You're just training
an image model.

00:39:20.100 --> 00:39:22.426
With text there are some
differences, some things

00:39:22.426 --> 00:39:23.550
that you have to deal with.

00:39:23.550 --> 00:39:27.690
So basically when you're
doing text models,

00:39:27.690 --> 00:39:30.390
the modern day text
models essentially

00:39:30.390 --> 00:39:32.100
consist of four steps.

00:39:32.100 --> 00:39:34.530
They do an embedding
where they're basically

00:39:34.530 --> 00:39:38.070
taking all of those words and
converting them into numbers,

00:39:38.070 --> 00:39:39.270
into vectors.

00:39:39.270 --> 00:39:41.520
The second thing they're
doing is that they're now

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:44.820
encoding these using an LSTM.

00:39:44.820 --> 00:39:47.220
And then, while you're
training, you're

00:39:47.220 --> 00:39:51.660
basically shifting the
attention during that sentence

00:39:51.660 --> 00:39:57.600
to basically focus the LSTM
model on a certain length

00:39:57.600 --> 00:39:59.430
so that it learns
to basically expect

00:39:59.430 --> 00:40:00.940
what's going to happen there.

00:40:00.940 --> 00:40:04.270
And then finally, in this
combination of this embedding,

00:40:04.270 --> 00:40:07.500
encoding, and then
attending, and then you

00:40:07.500 --> 00:40:10.350
have the prediction phase, where
you're basically predicting

00:40:10.350 --> 00:40:12.150
the next word of a sequence.

00:40:12.150 --> 00:40:14.250
But you're predicting
that in a conditional way.

00:40:14.250 --> 00:40:17.490
And then you're playing a
Beam search during prediction

00:40:17.490 --> 00:40:21.110
to choose among those
different possibilities.

00:40:21.110 --> 00:40:21.610
OK.

00:40:21.610 --> 00:40:23.460
So you have to basically
write those models.

00:40:23.460 --> 00:40:27.180
And deservedly, these
have this reputation

00:40:27.180 --> 00:40:30.270
of being really hard to train
and really hard to get right.

00:40:30.270 --> 00:40:31.890
So it's really,
really cool that we

00:40:31.890 --> 00:40:34.200
have a library,
Tensor2Tensor which

00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:38.100
basically provides this best
of the breed language model.

00:40:38.100 --> 00:40:43.140
And luckily enough,
it also runs on a TPU.

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:45.930
But unlike images, it's
not just scripting.

00:40:45.930 --> 00:40:47.254
We have to do some work here.

00:40:47.254 --> 00:40:49.420
So let's talk about what
kind of work we have to do.

00:40:49.420 --> 00:40:51.610
So let's say we want
to basically write

00:40:51.610 --> 00:40:55.450
a machine learning model to
complete our lines of poetry.

00:40:55.450 --> 00:40:59.840
So given this line, we would
like the model to do this.

00:40:59.840 --> 00:41:01.200
Is a model going to do that?

00:41:01.200 --> 00:41:01.700
No.

00:41:01.700 --> 00:41:02.640
Obviously not, right.

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:04.390
The model is going
to do something.

00:41:04.390 --> 00:41:05.970
But the idea is
to basically have

00:41:05.970 --> 00:41:09.960
it create plausible next lines.

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:12.630
And how do you train a
machine learning model

00:41:12.630 --> 00:41:15.750
to create a plausible
next line of poetry?

00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:18.580
What do you train it with?

00:41:18.580 --> 00:41:19.960
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:19.960 --> 00:41:21.399
LAK LAKSHMANAN: Real poems.

00:41:21.399 --> 00:41:22.690
You train them with real poems.

00:41:22.690 --> 00:41:23.731
So what am I going to do?

00:41:23.731 --> 00:41:25.960
I'm going to go look
at all of the poems

00:41:25.960 --> 00:41:29.920
that I can find, lay my hands
off on Project Gutenberg,

00:41:29.920 --> 00:41:32.640
and basically slurp them all in.

00:41:32.640 --> 00:41:35.620
And my input is going
to consist of one line.

00:41:35.620 --> 00:41:37.900
And my output is going to
consist of the next line.

00:41:37.900 --> 00:41:39.150
That's my label.

00:41:39.150 --> 00:41:41.560
And I'm going to basically
give that to my model,

00:41:41.560 --> 00:41:42.670
and say, this is my input.

00:41:42.670 --> 00:41:43.780
This is my output.

00:41:43.780 --> 00:41:44.470
Text input.

00:41:44.470 --> 00:41:45.590
Text output.

00:41:45.590 --> 00:41:47.540
Train.

00:41:47.540 --> 00:41:48.910
Fair enough?

00:41:48.910 --> 00:41:52.960
So in order to do that, what we
do is that we write a problem.

00:41:52.960 --> 00:41:55.600
And the thing is that I
recognize that my input is

00:41:55.600 --> 00:41:57.940
text, and my output is text.

00:41:57.940 --> 00:42:03.020
So what I have is a text to
text problem in Tensor2Tensor.

00:42:03.020 --> 00:42:05.420
So I have a text
to text problem.

00:42:05.420 --> 00:42:08.380
And I'm calling mine a poetry
line problem, because I'm

00:42:08.380 --> 00:42:10.240
predicting a line of poetry.

00:42:10.240 --> 00:42:14.680
And then I basically
specify how many words

00:42:14.680 --> 00:42:16.990
I want this model to remember.

00:42:16.990 --> 00:42:20.020
Now, I thought I would get
lots and lots of poems.

00:42:20.020 --> 00:42:22.930
But it turns out people
don't write that many poems.

00:42:22.930 --> 00:42:25.750
So all I ultimately
ended up with was

00:42:25.750 --> 00:42:28.060
about 60,000 lines of poetry.

00:42:28.060 --> 00:42:30.280
So it wasn't a very big dataset.

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:33.300
So I had to kind of
limit my vocabulary here.

00:42:33.300 --> 00:42:33.800
Right.

00:42:33.800 --> 00:42:36.460
So I did 2 power of 12 words.

00:42:36.460 --> 00:42:43.120
Just learn the most common 4,000
words in this poetry database.

00:42:43.120 --> 00:42:45.580
So that's the size
of my vocabulary.

00:42:45.580 --> 00:42:51.220
And then I need to write a
generate samples function.

00:42:51.220 --> 00:42:55.030
Given the data directory,
it's going to basically yield

00:42:55.030 --> 00:42:58.690
a dictionary that consists
of two things, the inputs

00:42:58.690 --> 00:42:59.880
and the targets.

00:42:59.880 --> 00:43:02.263
In my case, the input
is the previous line.

00:43:02.263 --> 00:43:03.721
And the target is
the current line.

00:43:06.367 --> 00:43:07.760
Fair enough?

00:43:07.760 --> 00:43:13.670
So that's code I write,
basically saying what

00:43:13.670 --> 00:43:16.430
my vocabulary is, what
kind of problem is it--

00:43:16.430 --> 00:43:17.720
a text to text problem--

00:43:17.720 --> 00:43:19.550
and that I want to
read in something.

00:43:19.550 --> 00:43:21.170
I want to write out something.

00:43:21.170 --> 00:43:24.570
And then I basically said, go
ahead and generate data for me.

00:43:24.570 --> 00:43:28.250
So this is, again, very similar
to what I had to do in images.

00:43:28.250 --> 00:43:30.980
I don't want my
TPU sitting there,

00:43:30.980 --> 00:43:35.390
basically reading 20
line poems everywhere.

00:43:35.390 --> 00:43:38.900
I want to basically create
this data in such a way

00:43:38.900 --> 00:43:41.850
that my input, output
overhead is minimized.

00:43:41.850 --> 00:43:43.460
And so what I'm
doing is that I'm

00:43:43.460 --> 00:43:46.800
basically running this
data gen, pointing

00:43:46.800 --> 00:43:47.970
at this is the problem.

00:43:47.970 --> 00:43:49.850
This is my data directory.

00:43:49.850 --> 00:43:52.280
So basically go
ahead and read it,

00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:55.870
and write TensorFlow records.

00:43:55.870 --> 00:43:56.810
Fair enough?

00:43:56.810 --> 00:44:00.470
And the reason it can do
this is because I told it,

00:44:00.470 --> 00:44:06.050
given my data files, how do I
create my input and my output

00:44:06.050 --> 00:44:07.421
from a machine learning model?

00:44:07.421 --> 00:44:09.920
Really when you think about it,
that's all it needs to know.

00:44:09.920 --> 00:44:11.060
How do I create my input?

00:44:11.060 --> 00:44:12.410
How do I create my output?

00:44:12.410 --> 00:44:17.180
And then it basically slurps
through all of the data

00:44:17.180 --> 00:44:19.250
that you specified
in this directory,

00:44:19.250 --> 00:44:21.860
and creates the
TensorFlow records.

00:44:21.860 --> 00:44:24.040
Once you have the
TensorFlow records,

00:44:24.040 --> 00:44:25.760
we are ready to train the model.

00:44:25.760 --> 00:44:29.060
But then I realized
that, hey, my model

00:44:29.060 --> 00:44:30.270
isn't going to be great.

00:44:30.270 --> 00:44:31.769
It's not going to
be great because I

00:44:31.769 --> 00:44:33.000
don't have enough data.

00:44:33.000 --> 00:44:34.880
Remember that first
graph I showed you,

00:44:34.880 --> 00:44:38.370
how much data do you need
for a from scratch model?

00:44:38.370 --> 00:44:39.780
Right.

00:44:39.780 --> 00:44:44.840
The typical Tensor2Tensor
thing, by default,

00:44:44.840 --> 00:44:47.810
is setup for English
to German, from all

00:44:47.810 --> 00:44:50.900
of speeches ever
given at the European

00:44:50.900 --> 00:44:52.170
Parliament or something.

00:44:52.170 --> 00:44:54.710
So a humongous,
humongous dataset.

00:44:54.710 --> 00:44:57.410
And I'm going to give it
60,000 lines of poetry.

00:44:57.410 --> 00:44:58.500
It's not going to be big.

00:44:58.500 --> 00:45:02.540
So what I want to do is to
basically make the model small.

00:45:02.540 --> 00:45:04.820
So these are my
hyper parameters.

00:45:04.820 --> 00:45:08.900
I'm basically saying, just
use two hidden layers.

00:45:08.900 --> 00:45:11.420
Just use 128 nodes in them.

00:45:11.420 --> 00:45:14.330
And filter them with 512.

00:45:14.330 --> 00:45:16.070
And my attention heads.

00:45:16.070 --> 00:45:18.560
How many words do I look
at at any point of time?

00:45:18.560 --> 00:45:20.540
Look at only four
words at a time.

00:45:20.540 --> 00:45:22.880
And do a lot of dropout.

00:45:22.880 --> 00:45:25.548
What does dropout do?

00:45:25.548 --> 00:45:28.072
AUDIENCE: Shuts
down the neurons.

00:45:28.072 --> 00:45:30.030
LAK LAKSHMANAN: Dropout
shuts down the neurons.

00:45:30.030 --> 00:45:30.530
Right.

00:45:30.530 --> 00:45:33.690
And the basic idea is,
it limits over-fitting.

00:45:33.690 --> 00:45:37.310
The smaller your dataset,
the more dropout you need.

00:45:37.310 --> 00:45:39.310
So because my
dataset is so small,

00:45:39.310 --> 00:45:41.530
I'm going to basically
increase my dropout.

00:45:41.530 --> 00:45:44.020
And I'm going to slow
down my learning rate,

00:45:44.020 --> 00:45:46.520
because I don't have a
great amount of data.

00:45:46.520 --> 00:45:48.190
So use a lower learning rate.

00:45:48.190 --> 00:45:49.630
And that's my hyper parameters.

00:45:49.630 --> 00:45:52.880
So other kind of small
tuning that I do to my model.

00:45:52.880 --> 00:45:59.560
And at this point, I can
just go ahead and train it.

00:45:59.560 --> 00:46:02.600
And the model is a
transformer model.

00:46:02.600 --> 00:46:07.660
And I have my hyper parameters
set as transformer poetry,

00:46:07.660 --> 00:46:10.180
because that's the name
of my Python function.

00:46:10.180 --> 00:46:12.650
That returns this
hyper parameter set.

00:46:12.650 --> 00:46:15.790
So I'll go ahead and
use that hyper parameter

00:46:15.790 --> 00:46:19.080
and run it on ML
Engine with one GPU.

00:46:19.080 --> 00:46:22.710
Or run it on ML Engine with
four GPUs, or eight GPUs,

00:46:22.710 --> 00:46:23.550
or whatever.

00:46:23.550 --> 00:46:25.420
If I wanted to run
this on a TPU--

00:46:25.420 --> 00:46:28.840
kind of wasteful, because
this is a small data.

00:46:28.840 --> 00:46:32.920
It trains in a few
hours on a GPU.

00:46:32.920 --> 00:46:36.370
But if I say, I don't want
it to train in 20 hours.

00:46:36.370 --> 00:46:39.080
I want it to train
in 12 minutes,

00:46:39.080 --> 00:46:42.400
I would basically flip
that, remove the GPU part,

00:46:42.400 --> 00:46:44.940
and add minus,
minus, or use TPU.

00:46:44.940 --> 00:46:46.210
And that's pretty much it.

00:46:46.210 --> 00:46:48.490
Right.

00:46:48.490 --> 00:46:51.460
And what transformer
is going to do

00:46:51.460 --> 00:46:53.710
underneath is if
this flag is set,

00:46:53.710 --> 00:46:58.360
take this thing that I'm doing,
and give it to TPU rewrite.

00:46:58.360 --> 00:47:01.270
That's all it's doing
underneath is basically

00:47:01.270 --> 00:47:04.099
calling TPU rewrite with all
