00:50:18.600 --> 00:50:22.290
So last bit.

00:50:22.290 --> 00:50:22.965
Custom models.

00:50:22.965 --> 00:50:23.850
OK.

00:50:23.850 --> 00:50:27.180
So we now know how to
create an image model.

00:50:27.180 --> 00:50:28.350
Just take an image model.

00:50:28.350 --> 00:50:30.180
Take a text model.

00:50:30.180 --> 00:50:32.257
We know where to go find
it and how to run it.

00:50:32.257 --> 00:50:33.840
The last bit that I
want to talk about

00:50:33.840 --> 00:50:36.314
is how about if you wrote
your own custom model?

00:50:36.314 --> 00:50:38.730
And I'm going to assume that
you wrote it using Estimator.

00:50:38.730 --> 00:50:42.470
If you wrote it with Keras,
you would basically do a Keras.

00:50:42.470 --> 00:50:45.060
After the model you would do
a Keras model to Estimator,

00:50:45.060 --> 00:50:46.560
and you will get
an Estimator out.

00:50:46.560 --> 00:50:48.960
So it's one function
call on your Keras model.

00:50:48.960 --> 00:50:53.730
After you do model.compile, and
then you do model.doestimator,

00:50:53.730 --> 00:50:55.210
and you have an estimator.

00:50:55.210 --> 00:50:58.950
So the idea is that now you want
to basically run this on a TPU.

00:50:58.950 --> 00:51:00.070
So what do you have to do?

00:51:00.070 --> 00:51:01.770
You have to enable
that replication.

00:51:01.770 --> 00:51:03.580
How do we enable replication?

00:51:03.580 --> 00:51:05.580
The cross shard
optimizer business.

00:51:05.580 --> 00:51:06.090
Right.

00:51:06.090 --> 00:51:08.510
We also want to
avoid host latency.

00:51:08.510 --> 00:51:10.454
How do we avoid host latency?

00:51:10.454 --> 00:51:11.620
AUDIENCE: Converting to TF3.

00:51:11.620 --> 00:51:13.350
LAK LAKSHMANAN:
Converting to TF records.

00:51:13.350 --> 00:51:14.820
Put them all in one place.

00:51:14.820 --> 00:51:17.429
You want to do static shapes.

00:51:17.429 --> 00:51:19.470
Now, that's something we
didn't quite talk about.

00:51:19.470 --> 00:51:21.510
We said we had a matrix
multiplication unit.

00:51:21.510 --> 00:51:22.710
What does it work with?

00:51:22.710 --> 00:51:26.220
It works with matrices
of a certain size.

00:51:26.220 --> 00:51:29.820
If you want to go multiply a 3x3
matrix, it's kind of wasteful.

00:51:29.820 --> 00:51:30.720
All right.

00:51:30.720 --> 00:51:32.400
So what we're going
to do is that we're

00:51:32.400 --> 00:51:35.310
going to basically bring in
a huge matrix to multiply.

00:51:35.310 --> 00:51:38.190
And that matrix is
a systolic array.

00:51:38.190 --> 00:51:40.590
It cannot be this
variable shape.

00:51:40.590 --> 00:51:42.630
It has to be a static shape.

00:51:42.630 --> 00:51:45.120
So what we'll do is that
normally in TensorFlow

00:51:45.120 --> 00:51:48.210
programs, we'll read batch
sizes with a question

00:51:48.210 --> 00:51:49.290
mark in the shape.

00:51:49.290 --> 00:51:50.520
We won't do that.

00:51:50.520 --> 00:51:54.960
In a TPU program we will
have static, fixed shapes.

00:51:54.960 --> 00:51:56.830
Normally it's not a problem.

00:51:56.830 --> 00:52:00.760
But if you have things like
language models, for example,

00:52:00.760 --> 00:52:04.380
you might be padding
each batch differently

00:52:04.380 --> 00:52:07.560
based on the longest
sentence in the batch.

00:52:07.560 --> 00:52:09.070
That's not going to work.

00:52:09.070 --> 00:52:13.105
You have to pad it to the
longest sentence in the corpus.

00:52:13.105 --> 00:52:14.660
OK.

00:52:14.660 --> 00:52:15.467
Yes.

00:52:15.467 --> 00:52:18.210
AUDIENCE: This restriction
because of sharding?

00:52:18.210 --> 00:52:21.660
LAK LAKSHMANAN: This restriction
is because of the fact

00:52:21.660 --> 00:52:24.720
that the speedup happens
because of the MXU,

00:52:24.720 --> 00:52:26.760
the matrix multiplication.

00:52:26.760 --> 00:52:29.400
And the matrix multiplication,
it's a systolic array.

00:52:29.400 --> 00:52:31.950
It requires a static
shape so that it

00:52:31.950 --> 00:52:33.660
can optimize this speed.

00:52:33.660 --> 00:52:34.500
OK.

00:52:34.500 --> 00:52:37.050
So you would have to do
padding of the tensors.

00:52:37.050 --> 00:52:39.280
So how do you do this?

00:52:39.280 --> 00:52:42.690
Well, what you do
is you basically

00:52:42.690 --> 00:52:46.470
use the TF data API
to read your data.

00:52:46.470 --> 00:52:49.830
And when you're doing a TF
data API, during training you

00:52:49.830 --> 00:52:54.780
repeat forever so that you
never have any leftover batches.

00:52:54.780 --> 00:52:57.240
All of the batches are
of a specific size.

00:52:57.240 --> 00:52:59.044
And then you read TF records.

00:52:59.044 --> 00:53:00.210
We talked about that, right.

00:53:00.210 --> 00:53:01.230
You read TF records.

00:53:01.230 --> 00:53:02.760
Don't read anything else.

00:53:02.760 --> 00:53:07.500
And you basically go ahead
and do a parallel interleave.

00:53:07.500 --> 00:53:10.320
So you basically read
them so that every worker

00:53:10.320 --> 00:53:13.150
gets its own copy of the data.

00:53:13.150 --> 00:53:16.020
And when you do your
pre-processing, et cetera,

00:53:16.020 --> 00:53:18.000
you do them in a parallel batch.

00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:19.840
So you call the prefetch.

00:53:19.840 --> 00:53:24.720
And then you make sure that
you always have static sizes.

00:53:24.720 --> 00:53:30.162
So if you have an evaluation
dataset that is 315,

00:53:30.162 --> 00:53:32.370
and you basically want to
make it divisible by eight,

00:53:32.370 --> 00:53:34.151
you drop the three
images or whatever.

00:53:34.151 --> 00:53:34.650
Right.

00:53:34.650 --> 00:53:37.560
So you do that,
and then you make

00:53:37.560 --> 00:53:43.120
sure that the first
dimension is your batch size.

00:53:43.120 --> 00:53:45.720
So you transpose your
input if necessary.

00:53:45.720 --> 00:53:46.380
Right.

00:53:46.380 --> 00:53:50.310
And then you basically
make anything

00:53:50.310 --> 00:53:52.500
that has a question mark
in the TensorFlow shape,

00:53:52.500 --> 00:53:54.730
you actually give
it the actual size.

00:53:54.730 --> 00:53:56.790
So this is basically
what this does.

00:53:56.790 --> 00:53:58.800
It basically sets the shapes.

00:53:58.800 --> 00:54:02.160
Anything that's a partial
unknown, you set the shape.

00:54:02.160 --> 00:54:07.380
And then you basically create
a TPU estimator instead

00:54:07.380 --> 00:54:09.090
of a regular estimator spec.

00:54:09.090 --> 00:54:11.470
Again, I'm running through this.

00:54:11.470 --> 00:54:13.670
But we'll try to make
the deck available.

00:54:13.670 --> 00:54:15.790
So you'll get that.

00:54:15.790 --> 00:54:19.110
And then what you saw
in my Jupyter notebook.

00:54:19.110 --> 00:54:21.990
You find the TPU with
the cluster resolver.

00:54:21.990 --> 00:54:25.260
And having found the TPU,
then you basically go ahead,

00:54:25.260 --> 00:54:28.290
and you train and evaluate the
way you normally do your train

00:54:28.290 --> 00:54:30.175
and evaluate.

00:54:30.175 --> 00:54:32.160
With this thing,
there's this idea

00:54:32.160 --> 00:54:35.689
that there are some functions
that you execute on the TPU.

00:54:35.689 --> 00:54:37.230
And there are some
functions that you

00:54:37.230 --> 00:54:39.510
execute not on the TPU.

00:54:39.510 --> 00:54:44.160
In general, all your input
and output is not on the TPU.

00:54:44.160 --> 00:54:49.150
Everything that is on the
TPU is all the matrix math.

00:54:49.150 --> 00:54:52.140
So with most machine
learning models,

00:54:52.140 --> 00:54:54.940
the model function
is on the TPU.

00:54:54.940 --> 00:54:55.440
Yes.

00:54:55.440 --> 00:54:57.350
AUDIENCE: Is there a
profiler so I can see?

00:54:57.350 --> 00:54:58.183
LAK LAKSHMANAN: Yes.

00:54:58.183 --> 00:54:59.490
There is a TPU profiler.

00:54:59.490 --> 00:55:01.560
So when you run it,
it will tell you

00:55:01.560 --> 00:55:05.730
what fraction of the functions
that they are running

00:55:05.730 --> 00:55:08.520
on the TPU are
optimized for the TPU,

00:55:08.520 --> 00:55:11.270
and what percent
aren't optimized.

00:55:11.270 --> 00:55:14.170
We try to shoot for about 70%.

00:55:14.170 --> 00:55:15.810
It's really hard to get to 100%.

00:55:15.810 --> 00:55:18.150
But we try to shoot
for about 70%.

00:55:18.150 --> 00:55:22.080
And the more you move all of the
pre-processing, all that stuff

00:55:22.080 --> 00:55:24.570
into the input function,
and you leave your model

00:55:24.570 --> 00:55:29.790
function pristine, the higher
that fraction is going to get.

00:55:29.790 --> 00:55:32.351
And finally, you save the model.

00:55:32.351 --> 00:55:34.350
And the cool thing is, I
want to save the model.

00:55:34.350 --> 00:55:35.880
It's just a normal model.

00:55:35.880 --> 00:55:38.760
And you can serve it either
from a CPU, or from a GPU,

00:55:38.760 --> 00:55:39.810
or from a TPU.

00:55:39.810 --> 00:55:40.510
OK.

00:55:40.510 --> 00:55:42.060
So you can export the model.

00:55:42.060 --> 00:55:44.370
When you export the
model, it exports it

00:55:44.370 --> 00:55:48.270
both in a TPU serving format,
and in a GPU serving format.

00:55:48.270 --> 00:55:50.120
So you can serve
from it either one.

00:55:50.120 --> 00:55:54.210
So all of these steps that
I just rushed through,

